Move scope-level rule-body checks to the Rust side

A new `scope_checks` module runs after `resolve_scopes` and collects
compile errors that need name resolution but not type inference:

- `scope_checks::bindings`: VariableIntroducedInThenStmt,
  ThenDefinedVarNotNew, MatchPatternArgVarIsNotFresh.
- `scope_checks::occurrences`: VariableOccursOnlyOnce.

Errors from both submodules are merged with `check_eqlog` via
`CompileError`'s `Ord`, so the existing kind-precedence ordering (e.g.
UndeclaredSymbol still beats a singleton variable) applies globally.

The corresponding iterators in `semantics::check_epic` and `semantics`
are removed so each error now has a single source. The eqlog-side
rules that populate the related predicates still run but their outputs
are no longer consumed; cleaning up `eqlog.eql` is left for a follow-up.
